{"id":"gt-2w9","title":"proxy: add config file with extra_san_ips/extra_san_hosts for operator-specified SANs","description":"## Context and motivation

serverListenIPs() auto-detects local interface IPs (e.g. 192.1.2.3 on a LAN).
It cannot detect the external NAT exit IP (e.g. 170.170.170.170) because that
IP is assigned to the router, not to any interface on the host machine. Querying
an external service at startup is fragile and should not be attempted.

Operators who want containers to reach the proxy via an external IP (with port
forwarding), a static hostname, or a VPN address that is not on a local interface
need a way to declare these explicitly. A config file is the right mechanism:
it is self-documenting, version-controllable, and does not require long CLI flags.

All work goes on the proxy branch.

## Design

### Config file location

  ~/gt/.runtime/proxy/config.json   (default; override with --config flag)

The config file is optional. When absent, all settings fall back to flag defaults.
Flag values override config file values (flags win).

### Config file schema (JSON)

```json
{
  "listen_addr":   "0.0.0.0:9876",
  "ca_dir":        "",
  "town_root":     "",
  "allowed_commands": ["gt", "bd"],
  "allowed_subcommands": {
    "gt": ["prime", "hook", "done", "mail", "nudge", "mol",
           "status", "handoff", "version", "convoy", "sling"],
    "bd": ["create", "update", "close", "show", "list", "ready",
           "dep", "export", "prime", "stats", "blocked", "doctor"]
  },
  "extra_san_ips":   ["170.170.170.170", "10.8.0.1"],
  "extra_san_hosts": ["proxy.mycompany.com", "gt-proxy.local"]
}
```

Field semantics:
- extra_san_ips:   additional IPv4 or IPv6 addresses added to the server cert
  as IP SANs, alongside the auto-detected local interface IPs. Use this for
  external/NAT IPs, VPN tunnel IPs, or any address containers use to reach
  the proxy that is not a local interface address.
- extra_san_hosts: additional DNS names added to the server cert as DNS SANs,
  alongside "gt-proxy-server". Use this for hostnames containers resolve to
  reach the proxy (e.g. a split-horizon DNS entry or a /etc/hosts alias).

Empty strings and blank entries are silently ignored.

### Why external IPs cannot be auto-detected

The NAT exit IP is assigned to the router, not to any local interface. net.Interfaces()
only enumerates addresses the OS has bound — so auto-detection via that API will
never find the external IP. Querying an external service (api.ipify.org) at startup
is fragile, adds a network dependency, and is meaningless without corresponding port
forwarding on the router. Explicit operator configuration is the right mechanism.
Document this in a comment in main.go near the extra_san_ips handling.

## Files to change

  cmd/gt-proxy-server/main.go   — add --config flag; load and merge config file;
                                    parse extra_san_ips / extra_san_hosts;
                                    pass them to IssueServer via proxy.Config
  cmd/gt-proxy-server/config.go — new file: ProxyConfig struct + loadConfig()
  internal/proxy/server.go      — add ExtraSANIPs []net.IP and ExtraSANHosts []string
                                    to Config; merge them with auto-detected IPs
                                    in serverListenIPs (or in Start before IssueServer)
  internal/proxy/server_test.go — test that ExtraSANIPs appear in the server cert

## Implementation details

### cmd/gt-proxy-server/config.go (new file)

```go
// ProxyConfig is the configuration file schema for gt-proxy-server.
// It is loaded from JSON and merged with CLI flags (flags take precedence).
// The default location is ~/gt/.runtime/proxy/config.json.
type ProxyConfig struct {
    // ListenAddr is the address and port to listen on (e.g. "0.0.0.0:9876").
    ListenAddr string `json:"listen_addr"`

    // CADir is the directory holding ca.crt and ca.key.
    // Defaults to ~/gt/.runtime/ca if empty.
    CADir string `json:"ca_dir"`

    // TownRoot is the Gas Town root directory (e.g. ~/gt).
    // Defaults to $GT_TOWN or ~/gt if empty.
    TownRoot string `json:"town_root"`

    // AllowedCommands is the list of binary names polecats may execute (e.g. ["gt","bd"]).
    AllowedCommands []string `json:"allowed_commands"`

    // AllowedSubcommands maps each allowed command to the subcommands polecats
    // may invoke. Subcommands not listed here are rejected with 403.
    AllowedSubcommands map[string][]string `json:"allowed_subcommands"`

    // ExtraSANIPs lists additional IP addresses to embed as IP Subject Alternative
    // Names in the server TLS certificate.
    //
    // Use this for addresses that containers use to reach the proxy but that are
    // not local interface addresses (and therefore not auto-detected):
    //   - External/NAT IP:  the router's public IP, if you have port forwarding
    //     configured so that containers can reach the proxy from the internet.
    //   - VPN tunnel IP:    the IP assigned to the VPN interface on the remote side.
    //   - Additional LAN IPs: if the host has multiple NICs or aliases.
    //
    // Note: the NAT exit IP (e.g. the IP shown by "curl ifconfig.me") cannot be
    // auto-detected because it is assigned to the router, not to any interface on
    // this machine. It must be listed here explicitly if containers need to connect
    // through NAT.
    ExtraSANIPs []string `json:"extra_san_ips"`

    // ExtraSANHosts lists additional DNS names to embed as DNS Subject Alternative
    // Names in the server TLS certificate.
    //
    // Use this for hostnames that containers resolve to reach the proxy, such as:
    //   - A hostname in the container's /etc/hosts
    //   - A split-horizon DNS entry that resolves to the proxy IP
    //   - A mDNS name (e.g. "macbook.local")
    ExtraSANHosts []string `json:"extra_san_hosts"`
}

// loadConfig reads the config file at path and returns a ProxyConfig.
// If the file does not exist, an empty ProxyConfig is returned (not an error).
// JSON parse errors are returned as errors.
func loadConfig(path string) (ProxyConfig, error) { ... }
```

### main.go — flag + merge logic

Add:
  configFile = flag.String("config", "", "path to config file (default: ~/gt/.runtime/proxy/config.json)")

Load config before applying flag overrides. Merge order: config file < flag.
Only override a config field with a flag value when the flag was explicitly set
(use flag.Visit to detect which flags the user actually passed).

Parse extra_san_ips strings → []net.IP (log and skip invalid entries).
Parse extra_san_hosts strings → []string (filter empty strings).

Pass them into proxy.Config as ExtraSANIPs and ExtraSANHosts.

### proxy.Config — new fields (server.go)

```go
// ExtraSANIPs are additional IP addresses to embed in the server cert as IP SANs.
// Merged with auto-detected local interface IPs by Start().
ExtraSANIPs []net.IP

// ExtraSANHosts are additional DNS names to embed in the server cert as DNS SANs.
// Merged with the default "gt-proxy-server" DNS SAN by Start().
ExtraSANHosts []string
```

In Start() (or in the serverListenIPs helper), append s.cfg.ExtraSANIPs to the
auto-detected IPs before calling IssueServer.

In IssueServer call, also pass s.cfg.ExtraSANHosts to the dnsNames argument
alongside "gt-proxy-server":
  dnsNames := append([]string{"gt-proxy-server"}, s.cfg.ExtraSANHosts...)
